def build(course, module):
    return course(90, 'Digital Innovation Design Practicum',
        'Digital Innovation\nDesign Practicum',
        'Present a fictional service design with a safe fallback, evidence plan, decision authority and a reviewable continuation decision.', [
        module('Frame and compare service options',
            'Compare a human-led route and a bounded digital option using the same criteria.',
            """
Start with the fictional Northbank help desk and one service problem. Requests reach the desk but some are lost before receiving ownership. A service goal is that every eligible request is tracked to a timely response through an accessible route. State who is included in the measure, what failure looks like and what is outside the scope of the proposal. [S71]

Compare a manual route with a structured queue and a narrowly scoped assisted draft. Use common criteria for access, safety, staff effort, quality, feasibility and accountability. For example, a queue may improve ownership tracking while adding a device access requirement. A draft may help organise notes while introducing a need to check dates, recommendations and attributed statements. Include the resources and staff time each route requires. [S75]

Olawumi's IJSDS article discusses cultural fit, community relationships and language access in Nigeria through a narrative discussion. Use these themes to frame questions about the local pathway, such as whether proposed communication supports the person's preferred language and realistic service options. Do not import its unverified statistics or treat its recommendations as measured results. [S78]

Write one requirement that follows from an affected person's task, one from service delivery and one from governance. A requirement can take the form of an observable outcome, such as a person receiving a neutral confirmation that preserves place in the queue. Record the assumption behind each requirement and the evidence that would make you revise it. A well-defined problem can justify improving the ordinary route first. [S76]
            """,
            'Northbank blames a lack of AI for missing referrals. It knows that requests sent after midday are unacknowledged but has yet to compare staffing, routes and queue responsibilities.',
            'Write the service problem in one sentence. Compare two options against access, quality, privacy, effort and ownership.',
            'Start with the missing acknowledgement. Compare a staffed workflow and a bounded digital option under the same criteria.',
            """
Frame a service problem.|Which outcome directly addresses the Northbank problem?|Eligible requests receive a traceable and timely response.|Deploy a generator this quarter.|Open more draft windows.|Increase character count per record.|A service problem describes what people need to achieve.
Compare options consistently.|What makes an options comparison fair?|Apply the same access, quality, effort and accountability criteria to both routes.|Count staff time for only one option.|Measure adoption for one route and equity for the other.|Pick the shortest proposal first.|Common criteria reveal the actual trade-offs.
Use local narrative evidence wisely.|How can learners use the IJSDS discussion in this exercise?|Ask practical questions about language, culture and community roles.|Infer the number of Nigerian agencies using a tool.|Claim the article measured the intervention's effect.|Apply one community model to all communities.|Contextual argument guides questions and requires local verification.
Specify an observable requirement.|Which requirement can be tested directly?|A person who switches routes receives a neutral confirmation and keeps their place.|Improve innovation across the field.|Increase enthusiasm about AI generally.|Guarantee satisfaction for everyone.|Observable outcomes can be checked against a test scenario.
Record what could change the decision.|What would prompt reconsideration of a portal-only option?|People in the supplied scenarios cannot complete intake on the available devices.|It uses a modern colour palette.|The team held a meeting.|The proposal has a short title.|A disconfirming observation needs to relate to the stated requirement.
            """),
        module('Rehearse exceptions and document safeguards',
            'Run a tabletop test of access, consent, errors, continuity and review responsibility.',
            """
Build a small set of fictional test cards: an internet interruption, an inaccessible field, a declined recording, a lost acknowledgement and a draft that changes the meaning of a case note. For each card define the initial state, expected safe response, responsible role and observable result. Include one ordinary case so the team can check that protective measures do not block the expected route. Use the same format for pass and fail observations so the team can compare them. [S72] [S75]

In the declined-recording card, let the person choose ordinary note taking and confirm what will proceed. The draft card can start with a request for a private callback. If the generated example changes this to a refusal of service, a reviewer should identify the discrepancy and preserve the correct action. Keep checks scoped to the supplied examples. [R82]

In the outage card, exercise the temporary reference, acknowledgement deadline and restored record reconciliation. Decide who will notice a mismatch and ensure that the person still has an access route. When mapping permissions and information movement, limit the data to the fictional task. A real service would need a responsible privacy review of purpose, sharing and safeguards. [S73]

Record planned and observed behaviour for each card. If a criterion fails, preserve the observation and assign a correction with a retest. The team can practise the manual route while the fictional design is revised. An accessible workflow needs real responsibility for exceptions, including a role that can pause the change and explain next steps to the person. [S71]
            """,
            'The trial team has a single successful demo. Its test cards lack a lost connection, a refusal of recording and a draft that changes case meaning.',
            'Write three exception cards with expected results, responsibility and follow-up if each test fails.',
            'The alternative route, review correction and accountable recovery must be demonstrable. Record a failed criterion and retest it after revision.',
            """
Include exception scenarios.|Which test adds evidence beyond the normal demonstration?|A request loses connection before confirmation and recovers through the fallback.|Repeat the successful demo unchanged.|Show the product logo from another angle.|Count attendees.|Exception cards exercise conditions that can disrupt service.
Give the person a usable choice.|What is an expected result on a declined-recording test card?|The person continues with the ordinary approved note process.|Intake automatically closes.|Staff create a separate unofficial recording.|The team removes any record of choice.|The test shows how a feature refusal affects the service route.
Catch changes in meaning.|How should a reviewer handle a draft that makes a callback request sound like refusal of help?|Correct it against the source and track the next safe contact.|Accept it because a model produced it.|Preserve the wording as a verified fact.|Escalate the person as disengaged based on the draft.|The reviewer is responsible for meaning and action.
Reconcile restored records.|What should the outage test verify after login is restored?|Every temporary reference is matched or assigned for investigation.|Only that the login screen opens.|That no staff remember the delay.|A total displayed without case status.|Reconciliation connects temporary work to recovered responsibility.
Record and retest failures.|What should follow a failed accessibility criterion?|A documented correction with a named owner and retest.|Delete that test from the record.|Assume all future criteria pass.|Declare success after a discussion.|Preserved observations support accountable improvement.
            """),
        module('Present a bounded launch decision',
            'Present a decision memo with evidence, conditions, ownership and a review date.',
            """
Begin the memo with the immediate decision. In this practicum the available outcomes are to continue a classroom trial, revise the design or pause it and use the fictional manual route. Give a reason tied to the goal and the most consequential result. A clear pause can demonstrate sound judgement when a feature undermines confidentiality, access or the integrity of a record. [S75]

Report performance in terms the reader can verify. Give the counts of eligible attempts, timely responses and unresolved cases. Include the route and version for each measure. Describe which evidence comes from the supplied simulation and what remained outside the test. A published adoption figure from England cannot answer whether Northbank's access or safety improved. [R83]

Explain what would be required before another decision. A proposed record review needs enough staff time to check meaning and an accessible correction route. A feasibility result can identify skills to practise, such as checking local language terms or managing an outage. Include a named role for observing any new condition and a review date when the evidence will be reconsidered. [S71]

Use a short oral or written briefing to make the decision accessible to someone who did not take part. Explain the goal, the person affected, the most relevant outcome, remaining uncertainty and the next responsible step. Reflect on whether participation and professional judgement were strengthened through the design. Keep a dated record of the exercise and the follow-up idea. Include the version reviewed so later results can be compared against a known design. [S76]
            """,
            'Northbank reports all 10 ordinary cases completed. Two interruption tests lost acknowledgement and one draft changed the meaning of a recorded choice. The team asks to expand.',
            'Write a five-sentence decision memo with evidence, one stop criterion, two revision actions and a named recheck.',
            'A bounded revision or pause is justified. Report ordinary successes alongside the three exception failures and define retest criteria.',
            """
Lead with the decision.|Which opening justifies a classroom pause?|Pause the proposed route because exception tests lost ownership and distorted a recorded choice.|Adopt the proposal because ten ordinary cases completed.|Defer the decision without giving an owner.|Claim the wider service is proven safe.|A decision should reflect the most consequential evidence.
Report exceptions alongside success.|What could be missed by reporting only the ten completed tasks?|Failures involving interruption and record accuracy.|The service's working name.|The presence of a slide title.|The calendar month alone.|Normal tasks and exceptions answer different readiness questions.
Keep adoption evidence in context.|Which claim is supported by an authority adoption study?|Responding authorities reported using AI in specified ways.|Northbank improved its referral safety.|Every local service should use the same system.|Northbank's draft is accurate.|Use adoption research to inform questions about implementation.
Assign a recheck.|Which continuation condition can be verified?|The named owner confirms every outage card has a tracked acknowledgement and every disputed draft is reviewed.|Everyone feels ready.|A supplier expects improvement.|Three weeks have passed without review.|Clear ownership and measurable conditions support reassessment.
Brief an uninvolved reader.|What makes the final memo understandable beyond the design team?|State purpose, people affected, findings, uncertainty and next authorised action.|Use internal abbreviations without explanation.|List only the software components.|Omit the unresolved outcomes.|A concise decision record should help an independent reader assess the next step.
            """),
    ])
